Formant Footwork

Ingredients: KYRGYZ TEMIR KOMUZ + CHICAGO FOOTWORK / JUKE + PREPARED TOY PIANO + DOIRA / WOODBLOCK + CONTRABASSOON + SHORT MONO BASS

Creative Catalyst: The genre began during a winter residency in a decommissioned speech-acoustics laboratory outside Bishkek, where temir komuz player Aizada Saparova was recording overtone studies through an old bank of vowel-analysis filters while Chicago producer Malik Turner programmed skeletal 160 BPM footwork patterns in the adjoining control room. The breakthrough came when Malik muted the dry jaw-harp signal and listened only to the narrow frequency bands amplified by Aizada’s changing mouth shape. Each tiny movement of tongue, jaw, and oral cavity caused a different overtone to flare while the plucked fundamental barely changed. Instead of treating the temir komuz as rhythmic novelty, he rebuilt the track so these shifting formants became the harmonic events. Prepared toy piano, doira, woodblock, contrabassoon, and short mono bass completed a style where the apparent chord progression is generated by resonance inside a human mouth.

Formant Footwork is a high-speed acoustic-electronic dance form built from Kyrgyz temir komuz overtone control and Chicago footwork’s rapid, asymmetrical rhythmic language. It usually sits between 154 and 166 BPM, but the harmonic movement is far slower than the drum grid. Kicks stutter, vanish, double, and relocate across the bar; claps may fall on expected anchors and then disappear for several beats; hats and rim clicks can run in sharp sixteenth-note bursts. Against that velocity, the temir komuz often sustains one physical fundamental while the performer reshapes the resonant cavity of the mouth to emphasize different upper partials.

The temir komuz is recorded with three complementary captures. A close small-diaphragm condenser sits just off the player’s mouth to catch overtone definition without excessive breath blast. A contact microphone on the frame captures the dry metallic pluck and mechanical buzz. A narrow room microphone captures the acoustic bloom around emphasized partials. The close mouth microphone is the primary harmonic source; the contact channel supplies attack; the room channel remains low except during long resonance holds.

Players favor a stable fundamental with controlled overtone emphasis rather than melodic pitch sequences. Mouth shapes move gradually between vowel-like resonances: narrow “ee”-type cavities highlight higher partials, open “ah”-type shapes expose broader midrange, rounded “oo”-type shapes pull focus downward, and tongue shifts create bright notches or sudden harmonic peaks. These are not treated as literal sung vowels. They are acoustic filters generated by the player’s body.

The central compositional device is the **Formant Ladder**. One jaw-harp fundamental remains constant while the performer moves through a planned sequence of overtone zones. A typical ladder may rise from a low fifth-like resonance through octave, twelfth, major-third-like upper partial, then into a bright clustered high region. The electronic arrangement treats each resonance zone like a chord change even though the string or tongue of the temir komuz has not changed fundamental pitch.

A second device, **Vowel Brake**, freezes the mouth shape suddenly after a rapid overtone sweep. Footwork drums may continue at full velocity, but all harmonic motion stops for one or two bars. The contrast makes the acoustic resonance feel suspended in midair while the rhythm races underneath it.

A third device, **Partial Drop**, removes all electronic harmony whenever one overtone becomes unusually dominant. Prepared toy piano, bass, and contrabassoon disappear for a short window so the highlighted partial can function as the sole pitched event. The track becomes rhythm plus one natural harmonic beam.

A fourth device, **Mute-Frame Ghost**, briefly mutes the mouth microphone while leaving the contact microphone active. The listener hears only the temir komuz’s dry metallic frame buzz with almost no resonant overtone body. When the mouth microphone returns, the sound appears to regain its harmonic mass without any synth swell.

A fifth device, **Harmonic Chase**, assigns selected drum events to follow overtone transitions rather than the metrical grid. For example, a rim click may fire every time the performer crosses into a brighter partial zone, regardless of whether the move occurs on a quarter note, eighth note, or off-grid subdivision. The effect is tightly composed but not purely quantized.

Footwork drums stay terse, dry, and fast. Kick patterns use doubles, displaced accents, silent beats, and sudden low-frequency gaps. Claps and snares are short, often appearing in clusters rather than on a constant backbeat. Hats use short sixteenth and thirty-second bursts but stop abruptly around exposed jaw-harp resonances. Tom or floor-hit samples appear sparingly. No trap hi-hat rolls, drill glides, or festival build structures are allowed.

Short mono bass uses root, fifth, octave, and flat seventh with extremely fast release. It rarely sustains through a full Formant Ladder. Instead, it punctuates the low resonance zones and disappears as higher partials become prominent. The bass should feel like a low-frequency stamp, not a rolling subline.

Prepared toy piano supplies brittle harmonic reference points. Selected tines are muted with felt, paper, or removable damping so the instrument produces short woody-metallic attacks rather than nursery-like sustain. Voicings favor open fifths, minor seconds, minor sevenths, octave fragments, and sparse dyads. Toy piano never doubles the full jaw-harp overtone sequence; it appears only at reset points.

Doira contributes dry rim taps, muted center strokes, fingertip rolls, and short shell noises. Woodblock provides narrow transient markers at the top or bottom of Formant Ladders. These acoustic percussion parts replace selected electronic hits rather than forming a separate folk groove.

Contrabassoon occupies the lowest acoustic register with breath-heavy fundamentals, short tongue-stopped notes, unstable low attacks, and slow falling minor seconds. It is most effective during Vowel Brake sections when harmonic motion stops but low acoustic pressure is still needed. It should never become orchestral or lyrical.

Vocals are optional and sparse. Kyrgyz, English, or Chicago vernacular fragments may reference throat diagrams, resonance bands, test vowels, calibration strips, or instructions such as “hold the cavity,” “third partial,” “freeze the mouth,” and “leave the frame.” Delivery should be spoken, whispered, clipped, or rhythmically shouted in isolated bursts. Conventional verse-chorus singing is avoided.

Effects remain disciplined. The jaw harp stays mostly dry. Narrow parametric EQ may be automated only to reveal naturally occurring overtone bands, not to invent harmonics that were absent from the recording. Very short room reverb is allowed. Toy piano can use a tiny plate. Contrabassoon gets a dark chamber. Drum buss compression is firm but transient-preserving. No shimmer, chorus, granular clouds, long dub delay, synthetic throat formants, or auto-tuned jaw-harp doubling is permitted.

The defining tension is rhythmic speed versus harmonic immobility. The drums can move at 160 BPM while one tiny metal instrument stays on almost one fundamental pitch, changing the entire harmonic field through the shape of a human mouth.

Genre DNA

* Tempo & Pulse: 154–166 BPM, primarily 4/4 Chicago footwork / juke architecture with displaced kicks, clipped claps, abrupt hat bursts, short mono bass punctuation, and high rhythmic density interrupted by deliberate empty beats.
* Harmonic Palette: Stable jaw-harp fundamentals, natural overtone series, fifth-like and octave relationships, bright upper partial clusters, minor-second tension from prepared toy piano, occasional modal bass anchors, and static tonal centers whose color changes through resonance rather than chord progression.
* Production Signatures:
    1. Formant Ladder — one temir komuz fundamental remains stable while mouth shape moves through planned overtone zones that function like harmonic changes.
    2. Vowel Brake — a rapid resonance sweep stops abruptly and the performer freezes one mouth shape while the footwork rhythm continues.
    3. Partial Drop — electronic harmony disappears when one overtone becomes dominant, leaving rhythm plus one natural harmonic beam.
    4. Mute-Frame Ghost — mouth resonance is temporarily removed so only the dry metallic contact-mic frame attack remains.
    5. Harmonic Chase — selected percussion follows overtone transitions rather than fixed metrical subdivisions.
    6. No-Synthetic-Formant Rule — the central resonance shifts must come from real mouth-cavity filtering of the temir komuz, not a vocoder, filter plugin, or synthesized formant oscillator.
* Dynamics: Intensity grows through faster overtone sweeps, denser kick displacement, brighter high-partial emphasis, harder frame plucks, shorter Vowel Brakes, stronger doira rim attacks, and tighter contrabassoon punctuation. Peaks remain fast, dry, and skeletal rather than huge.

Scene & Origin Lore

Formant Footwork formed inside a retired speech-acoustics and phonetics laboratory on the outskirts of Bishkek. The building retained vowel charts, dusty oscilloscopes, calibration tone generators, narrow-band analyzers, old reel machines, steel filing cabinets, wall diagrams of the tongue and soft palate, glass isolation booths, perforated acoustic panels, and long fluorescent inspection tables.

Temir komuz player **Aizada Saparova** had been using the laboratory to study how radically mouth shape could alter the perceived pitch color of a single plucked fundamental. She was less interested in folk melody than in the instrument’s microscopic internal spectrum.

Chicago producer **Malik Turner** arrived with a compact sampler and a library of stripped footwork drums. His first attempt chopped the temir komuz into rapid rhythmic slices. Aizada rejected it because the overtone behavior had disappeared. During a test, Malik accidentally soloed the microphone aimed at her mouth while muting the frame contact channel. The pluck became almost invisible, but a succession of bright partials remained. He realized the harmonic movement was happening inside her oral cavity rather than in the metal tongue itself.

Prepared-toy-piano player **Dana Ito** joined after building short dyads that could identify reset points without competing with the jaw harp’s overtone spectrum. Percussionist **Bakyt Omuraliev** added doira and woodblock as dry human transients. Contrabassoonist **Maya Benton** completed the ensemble with low acoustic pressure for sections where the mono bass disappears.

Their first performances were called **mouth maps**. A white projection screen behind Aizada displayed static diagrams of tongue position, oral cavity shape, and overtone numbers. The screen did not analyze her live performance. It simply gave listeners a visual vocabulary for the physical mechanism producing the harmonic changes.

The visual language developed around dental-chart white, analyzer green, tungsten grey, oxidized brass, dark wood, faded laboratory blue, matte black electronics, and small warning-red calibration marks. The unofficial rule is that if the arrangement still makes sense after replacing the temir komuz with a one-note synth, the composition has failed.

